---
import Docs from '../../layouts/Docs.astro';
import { WORKBENCH } from '../../data/workbench.js';
const toc = [{ id: 'use', text: 'How to use it' }, { id: 'benches', text: 'Benches' }, { id: 'checks', text: 'What gets checked' }];
---
<Docs title="Workbench" lead="Every Flint component rendered live as MUI, shadcn/ui and plain CSS, side by side, in every state. Where the three differ, that is a finding." toc={toc}>
  <h2 id="use">How to use it</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>Switch light and dark with the theme button in the header. All three implementations follow.</li>
    <li>Switch density on each bench page. Comfortable and compact change insets and gaps, never target size.</li>
    <li>Read down a column for one implementation's states. Read across a row to compare the three.</li>
    <li>Use the Live row with your mouse and keyboard. The other rows are forced states: they render the real hover and focus CSS without you hovering.</li>
  </ol>
  <h2 id="benches">Benches</h2>
  <ul class="link-list">
    {Object.entries(WORKBENCH).map(([slug, c]) => <li><a href={`/workbench/${slug}/`}><strong>{c.name}</strong><span>{c.lead}</span></a></li>)}
  </ul>
  <p>Usage guidance and code live on the <a href="/components/">component pages</a>. Which Figma components are built yet is listed there too.</p>
  <h2 id="checks">What gets checked</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Every bench page passes axe (WCAG 2.2 A and AA) in light and dark, at both densities, on every build.</li>
    <li>Every semantic and component token has a row in these docs; the build fails if one is missing.</li>
    <li>Token values match across CSS, MUI, shadcn and Swift (the parity check).</li>
  </ul>
</Docs>
